What this solution actually does: it is a local supervisor Windows Service for the two SWIFT *outgoing* MQ workers (CMS-Swf_OUT_MQ and CMS-Swf_OUT_MQ_GCMS). On every timer tick (interval from the encrypted central service configuration) it marks its own row as running in TOMI, reads the TOMI system date (stored but never used), asks the shared library whether today is a business day, and reads three parameters: the daily start and end time and SWF_OUT_GET_MT_BATCH, a portfolio-wide override flag. It then compares the machine's local time of day (truncated to the minute) with the window. When the flag is "1" it starts both services unconditionally; otherwise it starts both when "business day and inside the window" and stops both when not, in parallel, through ServiceController on the local machine with a 30-second wait. A failed start/stop is logged and written as a TOMI alert row. In a finally it marks its own row as terminated. It does not read MQ, does not build SWIFT messages and does not move funds; it is an availability dependency for the two outgoing workers. A healthy steady-state tick (service already in the desired state) is silent and quick, which is the main behavioral difference from its incoming sibling.
