SOCM-A1 - Schedule values are accepted as "any parseable TimeSpan"; a typo silently changes or disables the schedule
File: OperacionServicio.cs:51,57,95-99 (../../SWIFT/Outgoing/SWF_OUT_ControladorServiciosMQ/Aplicacion/OperacionServicio.cs) (parsing via shared ConversionHelper.ParseTimeSpanRequerido, ConversionHelper.cs:1339-1348)
Code block (csharp)
_configuracion.StrHoraOperacionesInicio = ConversionHelper.ParseTimeSpanRequerido(paramSistema.Value, ParametrosSistema.HORA_OP_INICIO.ToString());
...
if (!_configuracion.StrHoraOperacionesInicio.HasValue || !_configuracion.StrHoraOperacionesFin.HasValue) { ... throw ... }
Problem: the parser is TimeSpan.TryParse, so 8 becomes eight days (probe 2.2). A stored HORA_OP_INICIO = 8 is never reached by HH:mm, so both services are stopped every tick with no error, log or alert; a stored HORA_OP_FIN = 8 is never exceeded, so the window silently runs to 23:59. An inverted window (start later than end, an overnight schedule) never matches either. The guard at :95 only catches a missing row; a present-but-wrong value passes it. Fix: parse strictly as a time of day and validate the pair once, with named errors:
Code block (csharp)
static TimeSpan ParseHoraOperacion(string valor, string campo)
{
    if (!TimeSpan.TryParseExact(valor?.Trim(), @"hh\:mm", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out var t))
        throw new InvalidOperationException($"{campo}='{valor}' no es una hora HH:mm valida.");
    return t;
}
// after both are parsed: if (inicio > fin) throw new InvalidOperationException("HORA_OP_INICIO no puede ser mayor que HORA_OP_FIN (ventana nocturna no soportada).");
Benefit: a bad value fails loudly (and, with SOCM-A5, alerts once) instead of quietly leaving a SWIFT worker stopped. JIRA: SOCM-A1 (Major, P1).
SOCM-A2 - Pending, paused and timed-out service states are not handled; each tick can re-throw and raise another alert
File: ControladorServicios.cs:43-56,91-105,59-76 (../../SWIFT/Outgoing/SWF_OUT_ControladorServiciosMQ/Aplicacion/Service/ControladorServicios.cs)
Code block (csharp)
if (serviceController.Status == ServiceControllerStatus.Running) { return true; }
await Task.Run(() => { serviceController.Start(); serviceController.WaitForStatus(ServiceControllerStatus.Running, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30)); });
Problem: only Running (start) and Stopped/StopPending (stop) are recognised. A service in StartPending, ContinuePending, PausePending or Paused falls through to Start(), which the Service Control Manager rejects for a service that is already running or starting (documented ServiceController behavior; not executed here). After a 30-second WaitForStatus timeout the service is typically still StartPending, so the next tick calls Start() again and fails again: one ERROR log and one alert row per tick (see SOCM-A5). StopPending is also reported as true ("stopped") although the process is still shutting down, so a start request in the next window can race it. Fix: model the SCM status as an explicit decision and a bounded wait:
Code block (csharp)
switch (sc.Status)
{
    case ServiceControllerStatus.Running:      return Resultado.SinCambios;
    case ServiceControllerStatus.StartPending: sc.WaitForStatus(Running, espera); return Resultado.Iniciado;
    case ServiceControllerStatus.Paused:       sc.Continue(); sc.WaitForStatus(Running, espera); return Resultado.Iniciado;
    case ServiceControllerStatus.Stopped:      sc.Start();    sc.WaitForStatus(Running, espera); return Resultado.Iniciado;
    default:                                   return Resultado.Pendiente; // StopPending etc.: retry next tick, no alert
}
Benefit: a slow start becomes "pending, retry" rather than an error loop; real failures still alert. JIRA: SOCM-A2 (Major, P1).
