5. Architecture-Fit Note
This is a scheduled supervisor: one short tick that reads a handful of parameters, decides, and starts or stops two local Windows Services. Against the orchestration document's standing architecture question, modernizing in place is the fit; the workload is small, stateless between ticks and tightly coupled to the local Service Control Manager, so a queue-driven, saga or serverless model would add moving parts without removing a problem this code has. The sequencing rule applies: first fix the real defects (SOCM-A1, A2, H1, T1), then remove the duplication shared with the twin controller and the incoming sibling (SOCM-A7, A8 and GEN-4), and only then decide whether supervision should move out of a bespoke service altogether (for example to SCM recovery options plus a scheduled task, or to the hosting platform's own scheduler). That last decision needs facts this repository does not hold: SLAs for outgoing SWIFT delivery, whether operators legitimately start the workers by hand outside the window (the controller stops them within one tick), and the deployment and monitoring constraints.
